  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1) \n\nFirst round of storms have generally pushed through the region, with\nincreasing coverage of storms especially in the Tri State and central\nOhio areas at this time. This round has much with greater potential \nfor strong/severe storms largely through 22-00z. Keeping an eye on\nsingular/discrete cells and potential for rotation/a tornado or 2 is\npossible. Damaging winds remain the main threat though.  Flood watch\nwas expanded just slightly north as PW values at/just above 2\" are \nyielding very efficient heavy rain in the thunderstorms this morning.\nWhile overall storms should be progressive, repeated storms over the\nsame area has prompted the slightly expanded Flood Watch. \n\nRegional atmosphere able to destabilize following morning convection\nahead of the main shortwave trough over the Great Lakes, with \nmesoanalysis indicating the potential for SB CAPES recovering to 3000\nJ/Kg. With the main frontal boundary stretching from NW Ohio into \nnorth Central Indiana, the severe threat is diminishing for the far NW\nquadrant of our CWA. Best chance for convective development/severe \nstorms will be along and ahead of the pre frontal trough - \nessentially along/south of I70, with the greatest potential in areas\nnear/south of the Ohio River and south central/central Ohio. The \nmain threat with the 50-60kts of bulk shear will be damaging winds, \nand with 0-1km shear is 20-25kts in this area, a tornado or 2 can't \nbe ruled out. The main threat for discrete/rotating storms will be in\nthe early part of the storm event, largely before 21z, with the \npotential for a QLCS continuing through the region - exiting by 00z.\n\n\nKey Message 2) \n\nHigh pressure will build into the region behind the front for mid \nweek. Calmer/drier/cooler weather is expected into the late week. \nTemperatures will then begin to slowly moderate toward more\nseasonable values as we head into the weekend.",
